 `anliu.c`: bbr_policer (watching)
 
 Real network, t1>rb (30 Mbit provider limit, ~100 ms, 1% loss), scx: 53..72% lost at 1.6..2.0 s, both intervals app-limited at 46..50 kB/s while the encoder was still ramping, still in STARTUP. They started a test at 48 kB/s, which the encoder could not exceed, so it passed; every probe after it underfed and the tested rate stood (TUNING.md 55): 88% of the round policed at 48 kB/s, timely video 0.3 Mbit against ~17. The losses were STARTUP's own; early heavy loss on t1>rb showed in 5 of ~60 rounds. A STARTUP interval still counts as evidence, but neither the second interval of the pair nor the test can be in STARTUP. In simulation a 1 MB bucket at 100 ms gives a pair across STARTUP's exit (16.1 s in it, 16.5 s after) and keeps its timing: barring the first interval as well delayed confirmation by 8..10 s (video -2..-4 points in two seeds). In all 19 real policer rounds (g5>t1) the first test came after STARTUP. Simulation (policers 10/12/30 Mbit, 64 KB / 1 MB, 20/100 ms, 0/1%, 5 seeds; FIFO 20..200 ms at 0..15%; FEC on at 2 Mbit): unchanged to the byte.
+
+## 60. rto_queued_hold: an RTO behind data the path is still delivering
+
+`anliu.c`: rto_queued_hold
+
+A 2 Mbit TBF with a 16 KB bucket at 1 ms RTT (media_loss, FEC off, 940 kbit video): the bucket lets a burst out at line rate, the delivery-rate samples read 125 Mbit and burst_bw 6.7, so vq_ms drained the backlog at once and read 0 while a key frame queued 60..110 ms in the TBF; every key frame's tail went on its 30 ms RTO, 1100 video RTOs in 120 s, all of segments the peer had, +8% wire. The same at 170 ms (10 Mbit, 5 Mbit video): the estimate was right at 10.4 Mbit but burst_bw 17.6 (ACK dispersion of a burst the bucket front-loaded, kept at its maximum), key frames paced at 22 Mbit into a 60..100 ms queue, 143 KB in flight, vq_ms 2 ms, 2900 RTOs a run of which 2700 the peer had. The hold reads the ACK progress instead of the model: 2 Mbit / 1 ms / bucket 1100 -> 0 RTOs, wire 17.47 -> 16.05 MB (the no-bucket figure), video p99 97 -> 70 ms. The matrix (2/5/10 Mbit, 1..300 ms, 0..3% loss, with and without the bucket, 6 seeds): wire -2.2 MB a run on average (-18 at 10 Mbit / 1 ms / bucket), key frames +0.1 at 1..100 ms, -1.0 at 170 ms (10 Mbit, 2..3% loss, bucket: -6.7 / -8.1), video and audio on time within 0.6 points everywhere. The 170 ms cost is the baseline's RTOs firing before the ACK evidence for a frame's lost middle segments (111 of 120 key frames need a retransmission at 2%): a blind retransmission a round trip after the send beats RACK, which waits for the queue, by 30..40 ms - at the price of the copies. Exempting a frame's last two fragments from the hold (a tail probe) buys nothing: the useful RTOs sit in the middle of the frame (of 208 useful RTOs 27 were the last two fragments). Pacing bursts at the measured path instead of burst_bw halves the 170 ms bucket cost but costs 2..4 points without a bucket (the ACK evidence arrives later when a burst goes out slower) - not taken. RFC 6298's restart of the timer on every ACK of new data (tried earlier) delays the first unacknowledged segment too: the same key frame cost without the hold's exemption of holes.
